pigeoncam for Debian
=====================

Nothing is started or enabled by this package. Once installed, run as
root:

  1. /usr/lib/pigeoncam/bin/pigeoncam-setup.sh     answer the questions
  2. /usr/lib/pigeoncam/bin/pigeoncam-doctor.sh    fix anything it reports
  3. /usr/lib/pigeoncam/bin/pigeoncam-ctl.sh enable
     /usr/lib/pigeoncam/bin/pigeoncam-ctl.sh start

(This package does not put any of those three on PATH - see "Short
command names" below.)

The udev rule
-------------
/usr/share/doc/pigeoncam/udev/99-pigeoncam.rules.example is a reference
copy only. It needs your camera's idVendor/idProduct filled in before it
means anything, so this package never installs it as a live rule - doing
that for you would be a guess this project would rather not make. Copy
it into place, edit it, then reload udev:

  cp /usr/share/doc/pigeoncam/udev/99-pigeoncam.rules.example /etc/udev/rules.d/99-pigeoncam.rules
  $EDITOR /etc/udev/rules.d/99-pigeoncam.rules
  udevadm control --reload && udevadm trigger

yt-dlp: a deliberate exception to normal packaging
---------------------------------------------------
This package does not Depend on Debian's yt-dlp (only Recommends it,
which most installs will still pull in - see below). pigeoncam's
external live-status check needs a yt-dlp that tracks YouTube's frontend
closely; a packaged build that goes stale does not fail loudly, it
silently misparses the page, which quietly defeats that check. So this
project prefers the upstream standalone binary at /usr/local/bin/yt-dlp,
which pigeoncam-ytdlp-update.timer can keep current by running
`yt-dlp -U` as root, once a day.

That timer is installed but deliberately left DISABLED. Enabling it is a
real, visible exception to what packaging normally guarantees - a root
timer rewriting a binary outside dpkg's control - made on purpose rather
than smuggled past you. Choose one:

  * enable the self-updating binary, matching the upstream project's own
    default deployment:

        systemctl enable --now pigeoncam-ytdlp-update.timer

  * install Debian's yt-dlp package instead (already pulled in by this
    package's Recommends, unless you declined it) and accept that it
    will drift stale between Debian releases; or

  * update /usr/local/bin/yt-dlp by hand, on whatever schedule you
    prefer, and leave the timer disabled.

Either way, this package itself never writes to /usr/local/bin - not on
install, and not on remove or purge.

Where things live, and what survives removal
----------------------------------------------
  /etc/pigeoncam/       config.yaml, the stream key, and (if YouTube API
                        access is turned on) its OAuth credentials
  /var/lib/pigeoncam/   the rotation-history marker files, the local
                        archive, and (if created) the YouTube API venv

`apt remove pigeoncam` leaves both directories exactly as they are - only
the program files and systemd units this package installed are removed.
`apt purge pigeoncam` deletes both directories, printing exactly what is
about to be destroyed first (postrm). /usr/local/bin/yt-dlp is untouched
by either, since this package never put it there.

YouTube API access (optional)
------------------------------
Cleaner scheduled rotation, and automatic recovery from a broadcast stuck
at "Preparing stream", both need a Python virtualenv this package does
NOT create for you: creating it in postinst would need network access at
install time, which Debian policy disallows outright, and the OAuth
authorization step needs an interactive browser round-trip regardless.
Create it yourself once you're ready:

  apt install python3-venv
  python3 -m venv /var/lib/pigeoncam/venv
  /var/lib/pigeoncam/venv/bin/pip install -r /usr/lib/pigeoncam/api/requirements.txt

Full walkthrough, including the one-time interactive authorization and
where to find your persistent stream id:
/usr/share/doc/pigeoncam/docs/YOUTUBE-API.md

Short command names
--------------------
pigeoncam-setup.sh, pigeoncam-doctor.sh, and pigeoncam-ctl.sh are not on
PATH - this package installs the program tree read-only under
/usr/lib/pigeoncam/ rather than scattering symlinks into /usr/sbin, so
every command above is given by full path. If you'd rather type the
short form, symlink them yourself:

  ln -s /usr/lib/pigeoncam/bin/pigeoncam-ctl.sh /usr/local/sbin/pigeoncam-ctl
  ln -s /usr/lib/pigeoncam/bin/pigeoncam-doctor.sh /usr/local/sbin/pigeoncam-doctor
  ln -s /usr/lib/pigeoncam/bin/pigeoncam-setup.sh /usr/local/sbin/pigeoncam-setup

